{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966807,"nonce":"N8HKdFXRkE1i+bTX2+SNaiB3moU=","title":"use_state_keyed with per-open/monotonic keys leaks signals — keyed-state store and reactive","message":"`BlincContextState::use_state_keyed(key, init)` hashes `key` into a global `hooks` HashMap and mints a `SignalNode` in the reactive `SlotMap` on first sight. Neither has a removal/GC path (only `dispose_effect` exists; no `dispose_signal`, no `hooks.remove`). So keying transient UI state by a value that is UNIQUE PER OCCURRENCE leaks unboundedly for the process lifetime.\n\nConcrete trap: keying per-row hover/bg state by the overlay handle id (`format!(\"...-{next_id}-{idx}\")`). `overlay_stack` `next_id` is a monotonic `AtomicU64` only ever `fetch_add`'d — every dropdown open yields fresh keys → `options.len()` new signals per open, never reclaimed.\n\n**How to apply:** Key transient widget state STABLY + bounded (e.g. `node + key + index`) so repeated opens REUSE the same handles. To avoid stale carry-over from a prior open (e.g. a row left at hover colour), re-seed the base value with `state.set(base)` ONCE per open — do it OUTSIDE the overlay `content` closure (which is `Arc\u003cdyn Fn() -\u003e Div\u003e` and re-runs on subtree rebuilds; re-seeding inside it would clobber an active hover on a mid-open rebuild). Bind the visual via signal: `div().bg(\u0026state)` patches the Background property channel in place (feedback stateful is rebuild hatch, book: Reactive Property Bindings) — no closure re-run, no `Stateful` wrap. Found via adversarial review of the node-editor select overlay.","files":null}]}